  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/... \nHeading into the back half of the week, the influence from the \npreviously-mentioned low will continue to diminish, but sufficient-\nenough moisture for further rain chances will remain in place, at \nleast through Thursday. NBM/WPC continue to paint Eastern an \nNorthern Arizona in decent PoPs (30-60%), including our portion of \nGila County, thanks to orographic influences, but synoptic support \nwill be all but gone by this point, so desert chances drop back to \ngenerally below 20%, but not all the way zero. PWATs 100-150% of \nnormal are likely why non-zero PoPs are shown for the lower \nelevations, but unless you find yourself around enhanced terrain \nfeatures such as the Kofas or Table Top Wilderness, another rainfall \nshould not be counted on at this point. Gradual drying begin on \nSaturday, but it may not remain that way for too long as we may \nstart to see fringe moisture associated with Hurricane Polo by as\nearly as the weekend. Forecast confidence regarding where this \ntropical cyclone will progress in the longer term is low, and \nthere will likely be many forecast changes regarding it in the \ncoming day, but its potential influence will be monitored \nregardless over through the remainder of the week.\n\nIn term of temperatures, much like during the short term, not much \nfluctuation in day-to-day readings is expected to occur. Daily \nvalues will hover right around to just above normal, which \ntranslates to lower desert readings ranging between 94-105 degrees \nthrough the weekend. Overnight lows will be slightly more elevated \ncompared to where they typically are for late September, likely an \neffect of lingering moisture and cloud cover (at least for some \nspots), with readings between 70-82 degrees. Meanwhile higher \nelevation conditions will be quite comfortable as most locations \nshould be in the 80s, with perhaps a few spots touching the lower \n90s. Lows for these spots in the upper 50s to lower 70s will be \ncommon.",
